---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { appStoreUrl, APP_IDS } from '../../lib/seo';
import { GUIDES } from '../../lib/guides';

/**
 * Spelly guide: the spacing effect, applied to weekly spelling lists.
 * Research is named (Ebbinghaus; Cepeda et al. 2006; Roediger and
 * Karpicke 2006; Dunlosky et al. 2013) without invented percentages.
 * Spelly is described exactly as the product page describes it: two
 * recognition-based games, categories tiered by age, a daily challenge,
 * and a review that resurfaces missed words. It is not a dictation tool
 * and the copy says so.
 */
const guide = GUIDES.find((g) => g.path === '/guides/spelling-spaced-review')!;
const storeUrl = appStoreUrl(APP_IDS.spelly, 'web-article-spelling-spaced-review');

const week = [
  ['Monday', 'Pretest the new list before any studying. Say each word, the child writes it, you mark it. Keep the misses; those are the week’s real list.'],
  ['Tuesday', 'Five minutes: the missed words only. Say, write, check, fix. Add last week’s misses to the end.'],
  ['Wednesday', 'Five minutes: everything missed on Monday or Tuesday, plus three words from two weeks ago chosen at random.'],
  ['Thursday', 'Full list once, as a test. Anything missed gets written correctly twice, then spoken aloud letter by letter.'],
  ['Friday', 'The school test. No extra study the night before; the week already did the work.'],
  ['Weekend', 'Nothing formal. If a word from the week shows up in a book or a sign, point at it.'],
];
---

<GuideLayout
  title={guide.title}
  description={guide.description}
  datePublished={guide.datePublished}
  eyebrow="Spelly guide"
  readingMinutes={guide.readingMinutes}
  ogImage="/images/spelly-og.png"
  app={{
    name: 'Spelly',
    icon: '/images/spelly-icon.png',
    href: '/spelly',
    id: APP_IDS.spelly,
    storeUrl,
    pitch:
      'Two spelling games for kids, families, and English learners: Picture Spell to learn by sight at your own pace, Word Blitz to race the clock. 14,000 words in 23 categories, tiered by age, with a daily challenge and a review that brings back missed words.',
    price: 'Free on the App Store. Spelly Pro is a one-time $3.99 unlock. No ads, no account.',
  }}
>
  <p>
    The weekly spelling test has a familiar rhythm. A list comes home Monday, there is a scramble
    Thursday night, the test is Friday, and by the following Wednesday half the words are gone
    again. Parents notice it, teachers notice it, and the child notices it most of all, because
    the same word keeps coming back as a mistake. The test is not the problem. The schedule is.
  </p>

  <h2>What the Friday test actually measures</h2>
  <p>
    A test given a few hours after the last study session measures how much is still in
    short-term reach, which for most children after a Thursday-night cram is quite a lot. That
    is why the Friday score can look fine while the spelling in Tuesday’s writing assignment
    does not. The two situations are measuring different things: one is recall right after
    study, the other is recall after a gap with no study at all. Only the second one is
    spelling.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cramming is not useless. It is just front-loaded. All the effort lands in one block, the
    memory peaks on the test, and then it fades at the usual rate because nothing happened
    afterward to interrupt the fading.
  </p>

  <h2>The spacing effect, briefly</h2>
  <p>
    The observation goes back to Hermann Ebbinghaus in the 1880s, who tested his own memory for
    lists of syllables and found two things that have held up ever since: memory for new
    material falls away quickly at first and more slowly later, and relearning the material
    after a gap takes less effort each time. Spreading study sessions out over time, rather
    than massing them together, produces memories that last longer. Psychologists call this the
    <strong>spacing effect</strong>, and it is one of the most replicated findings in the study
    of learning.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two later threads matter for spelling in particular. A large review of distributed-practice
    studies by Nicholas Cepeda and colleagues in 2006 found the advantage of spaced study over
    massed study across many kinds of material and many age groups, and found that the best gap
    between sessions grows as the time until the test grows. Separately, work by Henry Roediger
    and Jeffrey Karpicke in 2006 showed that trying to recall material, even without feedback,
    strengthens memory more than rereading it for the same amount of time. That is the
    <strong>testing effect</strong>. A 2013 review for the Association for Psychological Science
    led by John Dunlosky rated both distributed practice and practice testing as having high
    utility for learners, while rating rereading and highlighting low.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of this says how much better a particular child will do on a particular test. It says
    that if the goal is spelling that survives past Friday, spread the practice out and make
    the child produce the word rather than look at it.
  </p>

  <h2>Why spelling is a good fit for spacing</h2>
  <p>
    Spelling words are small, separable, and easy to test. You do not need a worksheet or a
    curriculum to review a word; you need to say it and have the child write it. That makes
    spelling one of the easiest subjects to run on a spaced schedule at home, in sessions short
    enough that nobody resists them.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also helps that spelling errors are specific. A child does not misspell “because” in
    general; they drop the a, or write it with an s. Spaced review lets that one word come back
    on its own schedule instead of being buried in a list of nineteen words the child already
    knows.
  </p>

  <h2>A week that uses the list you already have</h2>
  <p>
    You do not need to replace the school’s list or argue with the Friday test. You need to
    change what happens on Monday through Thursday. Each session below is about five minutes.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Day</th><th>Five minutes</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {week.map(([day, what]) => (
          <tr><td>{day}</td><td>{what}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Three details carry most of the weight:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Pretest on Monday.</strong> Most lists contain words the child can already spell.
      Finding out which ones on day one means the week’s effort goes to the five or six words
      that need it instead of being spread evenly over twenty.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Write, do not look.</strong> Copying a word five times is rereading with a pencil.
      Hearing the word and producing it from memory, then checking, is a test, and the test is
      what strengthens the memory. Getting it wrong and fixing it counts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keep a running list of old misses.</strong> A word missed three weeks ago that
      comes back once this week and once next week is being spaced. That is the whole method.
      An index card or a note on your phone is enough.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>If the child hates it,</strong> shorten it. Three words for two minutes, done
      consistently, beats fifteen words for fifteen minutes done twice and then abandoned. The
      method depends on the sessions happening, not on their length.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Where an app helps, and where it does not</h2>
  <p>
    Be clear-eyed about what a phone can do here. The Friday test is a dictation test: the child
    hears the word and writes it. Most spelling apps, ours included, are
    <strong>recognition</strong> games: the child sees a picture or hears a word and chooses the
    correct spelling from options. Recognition is real practice. Seeing the correct form of a
    word repeatedly, spaced over days, builds the familiarity that makes a wrong spelling look
    wrong on the page. But it is not the same skill as producing the word from nothing, and an
    app should not be the only thing a child does with a list.
  </p>
  <p>
    What an app does well is the part parents find hardest to sustain: showing up every day with
    something short, varied, and not a chore. Spelly is built around that. Picture Spell shows a
    picture and four spellings, with no timer, so a child can work through a category at their
    own pace; Word Blitz is the fast version, where correctly and incorrectly spelled words fall
    down the screen and only the right ones should be tapped. Its 14,000 words are sorted into 23
    categories and three tiers by age, from beginning spellers to adult vocabulary, so a six-year-old
    and a parent are not seeing the same words. A daily challenge gives a reason to open it for a
    few minutes each day, and the app resurfaces words the player keeps missing rather than
    letting them disappear into the pile.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use it for the daily exposure. Use the kitchen table and a pencil for the pretest and the
    weekly dictation. The school list stays the school list; the difference is that by Friday
    the child has met the hard words on four separate days instead of one.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
